Raw dev notes -- Diving Deep into Document Drawings (and my sanity)

Okay, so this project, let's call it the "QMS RAG project," is all about making our internal quality management system documents actually *useful* with AI. Imagine trying to find a specific procedure or detail across hundreds of dense, technical documents, some decades old, full of diagrams and tables. The goal is to build a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system that can intelligently answer questions based on these documents. It's a huge undertaking because these aren't just simple text files; they're often PDFs with complex layouts, images, and even handwritten annotations.

This past week, or really, this past *period* (the commits are all from the same day, but the work felt like weeks), has been almost entirely research and documentation. We're in a critical foundational phase, specifically tackling the nightmare of "drawing extraction." I inherited a lot of context from a previous project that dealt with PII extraction from documents, which gave me a starting point, but the drawing aspect is a whole new beast.

My main task was to synthesize everything we know, or *think* we know, about getting meaningful information out of drawings embedded in these documents. This wasn't just a quick Google search. I dove headfirst into our internal knowledge base, pulling information from what felt like a million different repositories – 10 for one synthesis, then another 13 for more raw research. It was like being a digital archaeologist, sifting through layers of past attempts, half-baked ideas, and brilliant insights buried in old docs.

The hardest part was definitely the sheer volume and the complexity of the problem itself. When you say "extract from drawings," it sounds simple, but it's anything but. Are we talking about OCRing text *within* a drawing? Identifying specific components? Understanding the relationships between lines and shapes? What about different drawing types – schematics, flowcharts, architectural plans? Each has its own challenges. I found myself constantly asking, "What *is* a drawing to an AI?" and realizing how many different answers there could be. The "bidi" (bidirectional text) mention in the handoff notes also reminded me that even the *text* in these documents isn't straightforward, adding another layer of complexity to the overall extraction challenge. It's not just about getting the pixels; it's about getting the *meaning*.

Honestly, there were moments I felt completely overwhelmed. Staring at a screen full of research papers and internal wikis, trying to connect disparate pieces of information into a coherent strategy, felt like trying to herd cats while blindfolded. But there was also a strange satisfaction in it. Taking all that raw, unorganized knowledge and distilling it into actionable techniques felt like a real accomplishment. It hammered home the lesson that you can't just throw an LLM at a problem like this and expect magic. The pre-processing, the understanding of the source material, is absolutely crucial. It's a reminder that the "boring" foundational work is often the most important. It also made me appreciate how much collective knowledge is actually floating around, even if it's not always neatly organized.

Next up: Time to start prototyping some of these drawing extraction ideas and see if they actually work in practice.
